    "narrative": "The offensive outlook for Ghost Ride the WHIP centers on a high-upside trio in the outfield, with **Mickey Moniak** (75.98), **Seiya Suzuki** (75.03), and **Tyler Soderstrom** (72.72) anchoring our daily production. **Mickey Moniak** is our top play today, benefiting from a massive 14% park factor boost and a highly favorable matchup against Nick Martinez; his ability to exploit this specific right-handed platoon advantage makes him our primary anchor. Similarly, **Seiya Suzuki** and **Tyler Soderstrom** find themselves in prime spots against Lauer and Lowder, respectively, as both opposing pitchers present xERAs near or above 5.00, providing the high-floor environment we need to chase wins in a competitive Roto landscape.\n\nWhile we are leaning heavily into these favorable matchups, we must navigate the scarcity of stolen bases. **Corbin Carroll** (67.66) remains a must-start despite facing the difficult Casey Mize; while Mize\u2019s ability to control the running game drags **Corbin Carroll**'s score down by 4%, his projected leadoff spot and overall talent profile in our quest for elite speed keep him locked in. Meanwhile, **Brice Turang** (50.29) is a marginal play today due to the extreme challenge posed by Paul Skenes, though we keep him active to capitalize on his leadoff potential for run scoring. **Austin Riley** (51.86) remains in the lineup as a utility play, though we are tracking a 33% rain risk in his game that could disrupt his late-order production.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision involved our bench, where **Cole Young** (36.38) just missed the cut. While **Cole Young** benefits from a strong leadoff projection, the optimizer preferred the superior matchup data for **Travis Bazzana** (45.82) against Scott. The high xERA of opposing pitchers facing our starters outweighed the potential volume of **Cole Young**, keeping him on the bench to preserve our game caps. As we monitor the statuses of **Gabriel Moreno** and **Josh Naylor**, we remain wary of the \"Lineup Pending\" tags, though their current scores suggest they will hold their positions as long as they appear in the finalized batting orders. We continue to hold assets like **James Wood** on the IL, hoping for a return to health to bolster our depth for the stretch run.",
